 "answer-53f894cf-b4b4-5ea2-ac5d-69cb9357ba44" :answer/id #uuid "53f894cf-b4b4-5ea2-ac5d-69cb9357ba44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186305121 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+3{t}^{2}+1\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+L\\{1\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-2L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,L\\{{t}^{2}\\}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\n$$\n\n we can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{6}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-\\frac{2}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-78263033-1bff-5da3-9201-fff7488d909f" :question/id #uuid "78263033-1bff-5da3-9201-fff7488d909f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332828" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)=-{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{3{x}_{1}(t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-3L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\}=\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\n$$\n\nwe can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)=3{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)=-{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)-\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8b22932-e413-5c67-a864-5e79c72114a9" "answer-field-982ff96d-bb73-5c86-9b0f-d91a1d8bce